{
  "dom_id": "step-q47128",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258850/assets/4e4ca7af674b7747ca14a8ba08fb9bcb33d99950ba3ac9124ded9bd736847c54.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ are positive and grow without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
